__NOINDEX__ '''Reki Hakase''' ({{ill|暦博士|ja|暦博士|1=Reki Hakase|12=draft|12=draft}}) was a position within Japan's [[Ritsuryō system]], under the [[Ministry of Central Affairs|Nakatsukasashō]] and its {{ill|Onmyōryō|ja|陰陽寮|1=Yin-Yang Bureau|2=ja|3=陰陽寮|4=fr|5=Bureau d'Onmyō|6=zh|7=阴阳寮|8=ko|9=음양료|12=draft|12=draft}} bureau. The role involved the annual creation of calendars ({{ill|暦|ja|暦|12=draft|12=draft}}), revisions ({{ill|改暦|ja|改暦|1=Calendar reform|12=draft|12=draft}}), solar eclipse predictions, and education of calendrical students ({{ill|暦生|ja|暦生|12=draft|12=draft}}). The position was originally limited to one official (later expanded with an additional adjunct), with a Rank equivalent to {{ill|Junior Seventh Rank, Upper Grade|ja|従七位上|1=Junior Seventh Rank|lt=Junior Seventh Rank, Upper Grade|12=draft|12=draft}}. Over time, holders of the position typically held Ranks of {{ill|Fifth Rank|ja|五位|12=draft|12=draft}} or higher. The position's traditional Japanese reading is "Koyomi no Hakase," and the Tang Chinese name was "Saireki" or "Saireki Seihō."
== Overview == The earliest mention of the position appears in the {{ill|Nihon Shoki|ja|日本書紀|12=draft|12=draft}}, which records a request in the 14th year of Emperor {{ill|Kinmei|ja|欽明天皇|12=draft|12=draft}}'s reign (553) for the kingdom of {{ill|Baekje|ja|百済|12=draft|12=draft}} to send calendrical specialists to Japan. This request, made during heightened military tensions with {{ill|Silla|ja|新羅|12=draft|12=draft}}, likely reflected a quid pro quo for Japan's military aid to Baekje. Scholars suggest that Baekje preferred only to provide annual calendars rather than train Japanese officials in calendrical science ({{ill|暦学|ja|暦学|12=draft|12=draft}}). This reliance delayed the institutional establishment of the position until later periods, following the transmission of calendrical knowledge by figures like {{ill|Kanroku|ja|観勒|12=draft|12=draft}}.
In {{ill|Enryaku|ja|延暦|12=draft|12=draft}} 10 (791), the office received an allotment of three ''shō'' (units) of rice fields for its operation. Early holders of the post were often {{ill|渡来人|ja|渡来人|12=draft|12=draft}} families versed in {{ill|暦道|ja|暦道|1=Rekidō|12=draft|12=draft}} (calendrical studies) and {{ill|算道|ja|算道|12=draft|12=draft}} (mathematical studies). Over time, native Japanese families such as the {{ill|Ōharu no Ason|ja|大春日氏|12=draft|12=draft}}, {{ill|Ie-no-Miyako clan|ja|家原氏|12=draft|12=draft}}, {{ill|Ōnakatomi clan|ja|大中臣氏|12=draft|12=draft}}, and {{ill|Kamo no Asomi clan|ja|賀茂朝臣氏|12=draft|12=draft}} became hereditary holders.
Prominent figures associated with the position include {{ill|Ōharu Manomaro|ja|大春日真野麻呂|12=draft|12=draft}}, who advocated for adopting the {{ill|Goki Calendar|ja|五紀暦|12=draft|12=draft}} and {{ill|Senmyō Calendar|ja|宣明暦|12=draft|12=draft}} during Emperor {{ill|Seiwa|ja|Emperor Seiwa|12=draft|12=draft}}'s reign, and {{ill|Kibi no Makibi|ja|吉備真備|12=draft|12=draft}}, credited with introducing the {{ill|Dai'en Calendar|ja|大衍暦|12=draft|12=draft}} to Japan. The 10th-century scholar {{ill|Kamo no Tadayuki|ja|賀茂忠行|12=draft|12=draft}} and his son, {{ill|Kamo no Yasunori|ja|賀茂保憲|12=draft|12=draft}}, were notable successors. After Yasunori's death, his descendants retained the ''rekidō'' lineage, while his student {{ill|Abe no Seimei|ja|安倍晴明|12=draft|12=draft}}'s family carried on the {{ill|天文道|ja|天文道|1=Tenmondō (astrology)|lt=Tenmondō|qq= (astrology)|12=draft|12=draft}} tradition.
Private possession of calendrical texts was strictly regulated under the {{ill|雑令|ja|雑令|12=draft|12=draft}}, particularly the ''Hisho Genjōryō,'' which inhibited the cultivation of external talent. This restriction proved significant during periods of political upheaval, such as {{ill|Taira no Masakado|ja|Taira no Masakado|1=Taira no Masakado|12=draft|12=draft}}'s attempt to establish a rival court and the {{ill|Southern Court (Japan)|ja|南朝|12=draft|12=draft}} during the {{ill|Nanboku-chō period|ja|南北朝時代|12=draft|12=draft}}, neither of which could appoint a ''Reki Hakase'' or produce official calendars for their respective eras.
When the main line of the Kamo family went extinct, the {{ill|Kōtokui clan|ja|幸徳井家|12=draft|12=draft}}, a cadet branch, assumed the position. {{ill|Kōtokui Tomokage|ja|幸徳井友景|12=draft|12=draft}}, a prominent figure, passed the office to his grandson {{ill|Kōtokui Tomotsuna|ja|幸徳井友傳|12=draft|12=draft}}. Over time, the family's influence waned as the {{ill|Tsuchimikado family|ja|土御門家|12=draft|12=draft}} in {{ill|Onmyōdō|ja|陰陽道|12=draft|12=draft}} and the {{ill|Bakufu Tenmonkata|ja|天文方|12=draft|12=draft}} in calendrical studies gradually overshadowed their role. Eventually, the Kōtokui family focused on {{ill|暦注|ja|暦注|12=draft|12=draft}} annotation alone. Following the abolition of the Onmyōryō during the {{ill|Meiji Restoration|ja|明治維新|12=draft|12=draft}}, the position of ''Reki Hakase'' was discontinued, and its functions ceased with the adoption of the {{ill|Gregorian calendar|ja|太陽暦|12=draft|12=draft}}.
