When the main line of the Kamo family went extinct, the {{ill|Kōtokui Family|lt=Kōtokui Family|lt=Kōtokui clan|ja|幸徳井家|qq=draft|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kōtokui clan|qq=}}, a cadet branch, assumed the position. {{ill|Kōtokui Tomokage|ja|幸徳井友景|qq=draft|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kōtokui Tomokage|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}, a prominent figure, passed the office to his grandson {{ill|Kōtokui Tomotsuna|ja|幸徳井友傳|qq=draft|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kōtokui Tomotsuna|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}. Over time, the family's influence waned as the {{ill|Tsuchimikado clan|lt=Tsuchimikado clan|lt=Tsuchimikado family|ja|土御門家|qq=draft|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tsuchimikado family|qq=}} in [[:en:Onmyōdō|Onmyōdō]] and the [[:en:Tenmongata|Bakufu Tenmonkata]] in calendrical studies gradually overshadowed their role. Eventually, the Kōtokui family focused on {{ill|暦注|ja|暦注|qq=draft|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/暦注|qq=}} annotation alone. Following the abolition of the Onmyōryō during the [[:en:Meiji Restoration|Meiji Restoration]], the position of ''Reki Hakase'' was discontinued, and its functions ceased with the adoption of the [[:en:Solar calendar|Solar calendar]].

